**Shared component rules** | **Editable designs** | **Clear ownership**

{{ orgIntro(org) }}

Mangrove had evolved into a working [design system for UNDRR](https://mangrove.undrr.org/), with components documented in Storybook. The missing Figma kit was hurting adoption: designers could inspect the implemented components, yet couldn't use the same library to compose their own designs.

I used coding agents to build a Figma kit from Mangrove's component source and design tokens: named values for colours, spacing and typography. I defined what designers could edit and how to check the result against the implemented system. A core library is now published in a pilot, with an editable page example and a tested update to its spacing values.

## What was keeping the teams apart?

**The system existed in the developers' tools.** Storybook documented components and states, but user experience designers needed reusable assets in their own working environment. Looking at a component wasn't the same as designing with it.

**Five brands needed shared foundations.** The kit had to represent their theme values while retaining common component rules. Five unrelated sets of drawings would have given the team five more libraries to maintain.

**Shared names make the work discussable.** Designers can compose layouts; front-end developers can connect those layouts to implemented components; content practitioners can review labels, help text and content limits. I wanted those conversations to refer to the same components and rules.

## Why build an importer instead of adopting another kit?

**An existing kit would still need adaptation.** A React Aria starter kit or another library could supply foundations, but I would need to reconcile its styling, structure and editable properties with Mangrove. A wireframe kit could help with page composition while leaving the brand treatments to interpretation.

**Import plugins offered another route.** [story.to.design already generates variants from Storybook](https://story.to.design/docs/extend-stories). These options needed comparison on fit, adaptation effort and ongoing service cost.

**Mangrove's source gave me something concrete to reuse.** Its rules already existed as structured data and code. I wanted to test whether agents could help translate that investment into Figma.

## How did I connect the two systems?

**I kept the implemented system as the source of truth.** Token files supply brand values and references to shared values; component code and stylesheets supply structure, typography and layout rules. I used recipes to describe their Figma equivalents, then a development plugin to build variables, styles and reusable components.

**A Button makes the connection visible.** Its colours and spacing use variables, its brand values sit in selectable modes, and its label and state are editable. A designer can insert it into a page while retaining the library link that later updates depend on.

**AI helped implement the translation and its checks.** Agents followed values across source files, wrote construction helpers and tested repeat imports. For each component, I specified editable properties and selected narrow or long-content examples to inspect in Figma, using Storybook and browser measurements as references.

**I defined who owned each kind of change.** Component structure belongs to the source system; text and supported options in an inserted instance belong to the designer. An update that resets someone's label breaks the workflow even if the component still looks correct.

## What did real application checks reveal?

**A passing test hid a nearly invisible control.** The disabled ComboBox used an opacity value of `0.55` where its Figma variable needed `55`: a fraction had crossed into a percentage field. Inspection in Figma exposed the mistake, prompting a correction to both the conversion and the simulated test environment that had accepted it.

**Typography needed measurement.** A requested font weight didn't prove which font face the browser rendered, and small help text inherited a fixed line height that a simple percentage would misrepresent. I compared browser measurements with Figma to refine the result.

**Content exposed a layout limit.** A longer Table-cell label collided with a fixed divider, showing that editable text wasn't enough to support arbitrary content. I recorded that limit in the guidance for designers and content practitioners.

## What can the pilot now demonstrate?

**The library supports a separate design file.** Its components and supporting parts total 297 variants; a desktop/mobile resource-page example uses 56 linked instances with edited labels, optional content and page-owned notes. Designers can use those published assets without installing the importer.

**A spacing update preserved the tested design edits.** I changed a UNDRR spacing value from 30 to 32 pixels, published it and accepted the update in the example file. The layout changed while edited text, hidden content, selected states, brand settings and library links survived; restoring the value returned the measured fields to their baseline.

**The update test has a specific scope.** Figma propagates variable changes; I checked that the importer preserved the links needed for that to work. Publishing rebuilt component structure still needs a separate test, as do usability for new designers, rendering across all five brands and exact font equivalence.

**Adoption is the next outcome to measure.** I need to observe designers finding assets, composing pages without coaching and maintaining their work through real changes. The pilot demonstrates a working route; it doesn't yet establish that adoption has improved.

## What would I maintain differently?

**Generation created a maintenance obligation.** The reviewed pull request added about 77,000 lines, including roughly 33,000 lines of tests and developer audits. The recipes had become another component representation alongside the implementation, raising the question of how much tooling the team should maintain.

**I chose a smaller ongoing commitment.** I plan to retain token and style updates with concise guidance, while keeping the full construction toolkit for occasional new components or major redesigns. The [code split remains pending](https://github.com/unisdr/undrr-mangrove/pull/1319#issuecomment-5993683966), and manual edits need an ownership policy before a later rebuild.

**The bridge is a shared model in different working tools.** As with my work to [put analytics into editorial workflows](/work/2026/impact-story-undrr-analytics-overlay-mcp/), I carried existing rules into the environment where another discipline could use them. My contribution was defining that connection, testing its limits and deciding what the team should take responsibility for maintaining.

